Z||000008||RossT||05-12-2001||06:46 AM||||Scott if you do cut the coolant tube, take a look inside and see how much crud is built up. Just curious if the prestone flush really gets the coolant tubes clean or not. I have heard of people having to take the tubes off completely to flush all the crud out.<P>BTW did you pick up your other car yet?  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||BBBAD GT||05-12-2001||02:16 PM||||One suggestion is to take the thermostat completely out.  Chris took it out (along with drilling hole to relieve the pressure upfront in the rad area) before driving 500 miles to sell it to me (we met half way).  The theory is that the tubes between the front and back disipate the heat to a point that you aren't just pumping hot water all through the motor (as with no stat).  So far, I've had no problems with it, and runs 180-190 w/o the AC blasting (and with smaller 84 fan motor).  It does take a bit for it to warm up, so you might put one in there in the winter where you live, if you do drive that thing in the winter at all  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">        <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||maryjane||05-12-2001||11:33 PM||||BBBAD GT,could you elaborate a little more on "drilling the hole to relieve the pressure up front? What was the hole drilled in? thermostst? Did that to mine when it was overheating to & it helped till I found out I had fan relay problem. I remembered detroit diesels had a bypass hole cast in the thermo housing,always thought it was to let air trapped under thermo to bleed out of system.Bear with me, I'm trying to learn a little here. Thanks,<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 05-12-2001).]||||reg||5
